WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.210
You got John Rambo coming in

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.427
on this mission to rescue
these American POWs

00:00:09.427 --> 00:00:12.240
who are probably listed as MIA.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.032
There's no evidence at all
that there were any Americans

00:00:16.032 --> 00:00:18.512
still held after the war. None.

00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:19.680
My name is Bill Allison.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.140
I'm professor of history at
Georgia Southern University.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.330
I've been a battlefield
tour guide in Vietnam,

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.520
and I've written several
books on the Vietnam War,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.200
including one on Mỹ Lai.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.510
Today we'll be looking at
some Vietnam War movies

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.843
and judge how real they are.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.992
Spotted a large weapon down below.

00:00:38.992 --> 00:00:40.200
We're going to go down and check it out.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.970
All that's pretty accurate.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.880
The helicopters themselves
are the UH-1 Hueys,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.610
as well as the helicopter in the scene,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.580
the smaller helicopter, that's a Loach.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.360
So, Vietnam is quite rightly
known as the "Helicopter War."

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.450
Helicopters were used
throughout the entire conflict.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.763
That's actually Filipino pilots.

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:03.900
They borrowed those helicopters
from the Filipino Army

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.731
to use for this scene.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.960
Now, you might wonder why
they're attacking this village.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.010
It's supposedly a VC
stronghold. VC, the Viet Cong.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.700
Why do they have to get it over
this village into the river?

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.320
Why can't they just go up the coast?

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.598
If you've not seen the film,

00:01:26.598 --> 00:01:27.998
there's a river patrol boat they need

00:01:27.998 --> 00:01:29.356
to get on the Nung River.

00:01:29.356 --> 00:01:32.340
Problem there is, the tidal
flow is not deep enough

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.190
for the boat to actually
get across the sandbars,

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.657
and that actually is
real. That's a problem.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.550
Playing "Valkyries" just kind
of blew your mind, right?

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.010
Is that real? Probably less so.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.680
In at least one case
I know, Winston Groom,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.450
a veteran who did PSYOPs,

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.150
he remembered flying around at night

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.125
playing Vietnamese funeral music

00:02:00.125 --> 00:02:02.441
to try to freak out the VC.

00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:04.800
Was it effective? I would think not.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.120
And I think part of that

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.880
is making an assumption
about the Vietnamese,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.530
that they're not as smart,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.780
that they would be
fallible to superstition

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.038
and things like that.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.450
OK, there's napalm at work.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.320
Napalm is ubiquitous in the
Vietnam War. It's very common.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.020
It actually is derived from World War II.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.310
Give you a point of comparison.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.940
Probably about 30,000 tons
of napalm was used in Korea.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.960
Almost 400,000 tons of napalm
was dropped in Vietnam.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.520
So, it's highly flammable.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.040
It's kind of like a gelatinous substance.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.700
So if it gets on you and
burning, it sticks to you.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.860
You know, most people don't survive it,

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.380
but it's designed to destroy cover,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.120
and if enemy are in there,

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.930
then from your perspective
of using it, all the better.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.405
Which is pretty horrific.
It's a horrific weapon.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.770
I love the smell of napalm in the morning.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.300
Napalm does smell.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.830
Obviously, with everything that's in it,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.520
it's going to have a very
gasoline-y, petroleum-like smell.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.660
So it has a very distinct
odor to it, for sure.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.700
Now, there's Robert
Duvall as Col. Kilgore,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.430
probably one of the most iconic characters

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.140
in any Vietnam film.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.710
He is based on a guy, in part
by a guy, named John Stockton,

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.980
who was the commander of the
unit that's depicted here,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.290
the 9th Cav of the 1st Cavalry Division.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.830
As far as it being a piece of
film, I'd give it a 10. Right?

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.470
In reality, eh, you know,

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.450
it's a little ludicrous
on a lot of levels.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.659
So I'm guessing probably about a five.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.140
The PAVN, also known as the NVA,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.280
the North Vietnamese Army,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.500
so it's the People's Army of Vietnam.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.800
The PAVN getting ready, I
think that's pretty accurate.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.270
What this scene is depicting is

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:01.830
the January 1st, 1968, attack
on Fire Support Base Burt,

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.570
which is near the Cambodian border.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.536
It's part of the 25th Infantry Division's

00:04:05.536 --> 00:04:06.870
area of operations.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.090
Oliver Stone, the director of "Platoon,"

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.490
was in the 25th Infantry Division

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.620
and was involved in these engagements.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.530
And so that weapon they put
in, lodged in the tree there,

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.340
it's in essence a marker
for the VC and PAVN guys

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.660
to know where they're at.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.010
So if they have to go back,

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.930
they'll see that marker in the tree.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.547
They'll have a waypoint.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.690
This is an outer defensive perimeter,

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:40.440
and they've got trip wires
set up for flares to go out,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.706
and that tells you somebody's out there.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:46.613
There!

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.310
OK, what Charlie Sheen just did there was

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:51.840
fire the Claymores.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.340
So you've got these Claymore mines

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.020
wired out along the perimeter,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.610
so when the enemy comes through,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.670
they're explosive and they
shoot out a bunch of shrapnel.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.110
It's a clicker.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:05.820
And so you try to bang
it so that it will fire.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.120
So, the bunkers that you see
Charlie Sheen in and others,

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.430
if you had the time to
cut down a couple of trees

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.230
and put some logs on top of you,

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.016
that's a good defensive position to be in.

00:05:16.016 --> 00:05:18.495
Soldier: Aah!

00:05:18.495 --> 00:05:19.392
Aah!

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.253
You had a command bunker,
which the guy runs into

00:05:23.253 --> 00:05:25.020
with the grenade, blows it up, right?

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.820
I'm like, "Good luck with that."

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.130
One, you gotta know where it's at.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.140
Two, you gotta be able to see it.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.610
It's dark except for

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.550
the flashing of different
explosions and stuff,

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:36.780
which means you're going to have trouble

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.610
maintaining your night vision.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:43.560
So that probably more likely
could have been hit by mortars

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:46.487
or something like that from the enemy.

00:05:46.487 --> 00:05:49.602
Dump everything you've got left on my pos.

00:05:49.602 --> 00:05:52.244
Helicopter pilot: We are
coming cocked for treetops.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.770
OK, this is a really desperate act.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.430
To call everything remaining,
everything up in the air,

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.060
the problem with that is

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:03.930
you're going to kill a
lot of your own guys.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.140
And how often that happened
in Vietnam? Very rarely.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:11.790
So, a fire support base is
actually, it's a small post,

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.910
but it's very organized,
very well defended.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:17.220
What's depicted here is not that,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.711
and so the VC are hitting it

00:06:20.711 --> 00:06:22.961
before it's well defended.

00:06:22.961 --> 00:06:24.090
And the actual battle,

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.420
I'm not sure that's
actually that realistic.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.883
If you watch the scene in its totality,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.707
you know, I'd have to give it a six maybe.

00:06:35.707 --> 00:06:37.500
As far as the actual real battle,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.017
I think it's a little overplayed.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.770
Tunnels were kind of
the go-to fortification,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.420
especially for the Viet Cong,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.557
but for the PAVN, that part, I don't know.

00:06:54.557 --> 00:06:55.860
I could see where

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.700
they would have entrenched
bunkers probably,

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:04.050
but to spend the time to
dig an entire complex,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.150
like was depicted in this scene,

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.356
seems to me a bit of a stretch.

00:07:08.356 --> 00:07:12.540
VC would do that because the
VC usually are locally based,

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.010
not moving around as much.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.080
They have an operating
area and they're going out.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.227
PAVN, they're moving around.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.238
So, this is where

00:07:25.238 --> 00:07:28.504
we're really infatuated
with the helicopter

00:07:28.504 --> 00:07:31.590
as a way to bring lots
of troops into an area

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:34.050
that otherwise you can't really get to.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:36.480
And the idea is an element of surprise.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.150
Although a bunch of
helicopters coming at you

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.080
isn't really surprising

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.030
because you can hear
them from a long way off.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:43.380
So, this is depicting

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.770
the November 1965 battle
of the Ia Drăng Valley.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.920
This is really the first
time where American forces

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.270
face off against the People's
Army of Vietnam, the PAVN.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.030
Like, tête-à-tête. Right?

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.850
This is part of Americanizing the war

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.970
that the Johnson
administration; the Pentagon;

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.250
MACV, Military Assistance
Command, Vietnam;

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:07.860
determine that the ARVN,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.310
the Army of the Republic of Vietnam,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.872
is not quite ready to fight on its own.

00:08:16.046 --> 00:08:19.230
This is a PAVN operation.
Those were clearly Viet Cong.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:20.063
They had the hat, you know,

00:08:20.063 --> 00:08:23.113
the conical hat, the black, right?

00:08:31.299 --> 00:08:33.930
OK, so the way the PAVN reacts,

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:37.020
it seems like there's
just mass wave assault,

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.730
and that's actually kind of accurate.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:42.540
The battle of Điện Biên Phủ
in 1954 against the French,

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:46.919
Gen. Giáp just ordered wave after wave

00:08:46.919 --> 00:08:48.930
of these costly assaults.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.750
I mean, they won the battle,
they defeated the French,

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.550
but at great cost.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:55.830
And Giáp was criticized for this.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.850
But this kind of stays in
the doctrine, if you will,

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.640
for the PAVN, of, "This
is how you do things."

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.710
And in this scene,

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:09.270
part of the tactic is to get
as close to the Americans

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.830
or to your enemy as possible.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.350
So if you can get close and hug your enemy

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.725
as quick as possible, that
makes it much more difficult

00:09:16.725 --> 00:09:18.510
to call in the air strikes.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:20.940
But they're learning. This is early.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:22.710
We're talking 1965 here.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:27.690
So I don't think the PAVN
is fully appreciative of

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.150
the capability of American air power,

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.430
how quickly it can respond.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.005
Broken arrow!

00:09:34.005 --> 00:09:35.330
Broken arrow!

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.313
At 150!

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:40.530
OK, "broken arrow."

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:43.020
For a lot of us, if we
heard "broken arrow,"

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.720
we would say, "Well, Hal
Moore lost a nuclear weapon?

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.160
What's going on there?"

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.740
Because that's what the code word is for,

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.140
some incident with a nuclear weapon.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:55.050
"Broken arrow" may have been the code word

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:57.300
for that particular operation

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:59.430
if they were in danger of being overrun.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.350
What happens when you call "broken arrow,"

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.500
all air assets in the
area, whatever's up there,

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.050
is supposed to converge on your position

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:10.380
and drop on the coordinates that you give.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.450
And that's what happens here.

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.130
And that's real,

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:17.220
but it's not Hal Moore going,
"Broken arrow. Broken arrow."

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.300
According to the book,

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:21.180
it was the forward air controller.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:24.497
Minor inaccuracy. But Mel Gibson
has to be the hero, right?

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:31.860
So, there's your napalm.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.626
And in one of these
attacks, it is true that

00:10:34.626 --> 00:10:38.100
the napalm was dropped so
close to Moore's perimeter

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:41.160
that a couple of American
soldiers were actually burned

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.020
in the napalm attack and killed.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:46.392
I know a lot of people
who really like this film

00:10:46.392 --> 00:10:47.520
because it's dramatic,

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.460
and if they've read the book especially,

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.923
but this attack, it just,

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.670
it fails on a lot of levels, I think.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.340
I'd give it a five.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:02.130
Look out there, Sergeant.
Can you see them?

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:03.540
They got rifles.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:04.840
Can you see the rifles?

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.140
This is a bad situation.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.390
We've totally lost
command and control here.

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:15.090
He didn't want them to fire,
but someone started firing.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:17.430
The lieutenant there, he's clearly lost it

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:19.410
because he's seeing
things that aren't there.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:22.920
So he's seeing VC running around,

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:26.010
and he can see their SKSs or AK-47s.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.897
Tom Cruise's character
is looking and going,

00:11:28.897 --> 00:11:30.570
"I don't really see that."

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:33.453
You've got bad leadership at the top.

00:11:34.702 --> 00:11:36.600
A guy makes a bad assumption.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.992
Then you have lack of command and control,

00:11:38.992 --> 00:11:40.740
discipline over your own troops.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.233
This is not outside the
realm of possibility.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:47.903
Soldier: We didn't do this, did we?

00:11:49.172 --> 00:11:52.110
Oh, my God. Jesus Christ.

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:54.930
This just shows how things can go wrong

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.180
and a lot of civilians
get hurt and killed.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:00.540
So, if this village had
been a VC stronghold

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:02.760
and there had been actual
Viet Cong in there,

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:05.610
clearly identifiable and everything,

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:08.400
no doubt they would've
fired into the village

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:10.560
and then swept into the village.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.530
This is not so much like a
search-and-destroy mission,

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:15.240
it's more of a sweep and clear.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:19.020
Search and destroy, which
is a pretty common tactic

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:21.750
for the Americans in the Vietnam War,

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.900
is basically go out,
try to find the enemy,

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:26.670
make contact with the enemy,

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:29.580
then call in firepower to destroy them.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:30.720
Literally kill them.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:32.880
But when you do things
like this in the scene

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:34.950
and you kill a bunch of innocents,

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.860
that sets you back a lot
with the local population.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.480
But there were instances

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:42.510
where villages were attacked innocently.

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:44.880
So, for example, the Mỹ Lai massacre,

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.343
which occurred in March of 1968,

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:50.640
when a company of American troops,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.800
as part of Task Force Barker,

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.050
went into the Sơn Mỹ village complex,

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.260
and specifically Mỹ Lai itself,

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:02.400
expecting to find an entire
battalion of Viet Cong,

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:03.570
and they get in there,

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:07.860
and there's no 48th Local Force Battalion.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:08.910
It's not there.

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:10.620
The villagers are there.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.810
But they're so amped up that they just,

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:15.480
some immediately assume
that they're all VC.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:16.350
By the end of the day,

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:20.220
between Mỹ Lai and a couple
of other places in that area,

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:24.000
well over 500 men, women,
and children were killed.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.980
But Mỹ Lai is a rarity. It's an exception.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:27.630
I'd give it an eight.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:31.890
It shows just how a
couple of bad decisions

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:34.881
or happenstance can cause

00:13:34.881 --> 00:13:37.806
a tragedy in war.

00:13:37.806 --> 00:13:42.467
And unfortunately, war is all
about tragedies happening.

00:13:46.732 --> 00:13:48.170
Rambo: Go on! Get moving!

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:50.602
So, the setup here is you
got John Rambo coming in

00:13:50.602 --> 00:13:53.835
on this mission to rescue
these American POWs

00:13:53.835 --> 00:13:56.610
who are probably listed as MIA.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.970
The war's over and there's
still Americans being held

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.280
by the nasty Vietnamese, right?

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:06.005
There's no evidence at all
that there were any Americans

00:14:06.005 --> 00:14:08.688
still held after the war. None.

00:14:08.688 --> 00:14:10.110
But after the war,

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.300
there became a cottage industry in Vietnam

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:16.380
and especially in Thailand
of selling false information

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:19.230
to families who had
people listed as missing.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.910
And all those missing names are listed

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:22.350
in The New York Times.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:24.990
They know who they are. They
know the units and everything.

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:26.850
They can create fake dog tags

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:28.560
and rust them up and everything.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:32.760
And you'll pay, you know,
$10,000 to somebody in Bangkok

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:35.160
who has this information
on your loved one, right?

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:36.210
In the hope of.

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:38.370
Ross Perot got hung up in this,

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:39.630
trying to rescue people again.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:43.530
Chuck Norris made six
films about going back

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:44.820
and rescuing these people.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:46.950
We get our history from movies.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.710
So we all come to believe
that this is the case, right?

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:52.952
It's a tragic deal all
the way around, I think.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:58.710
When this film comes out, you
know, height of the Cold War,

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:00.510
you gotta have the
Russians in there, right?

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:03.150
Now, would a Russian have
been out there at this prison?

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:06.757
Probably not. Thousands
of Russian and Chinese

00:15:06.757 --> 00:15:11.580
technical advisors, military
advisors were in North Vietnam

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.140
throughout the whole conflict.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:17.280
A lot of them were there
to train, for example,

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:19.800
North Vietnamese antiaircraft crews

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.710
to operate these Soviet and Chinese

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.110
fairly sophisticated antiaircraft weapons.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.920
But they also have operational
and strategic advisors.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:30.420
So that's not uncommon at all.

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:32.542
I gotta like a guy who's muscled like that

00:15:32.542 --> 00:15:35.460
and can operate an M60 with impunity

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.950
and kill pretty much everyone around him.

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:42.510
But as far as reality goes, zilch.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:43.685
Gotta give it a zero.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.530
OK, let's set up this scene.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.150
This is from "The Green Berets,"

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:57.960
and it was filmed in '66,
'67, released in June of 1968.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.180
So this is after the Tết Offensive,

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:02.220
after the New Hampshire primary,

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:05.040
after Martin Luther
King's been assassinated,

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:07.230
Johnson's announced he's
not going to run again.

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:09.060
This is not the best time to release

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:11.490
a go, rah, Vietnam film.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:14.773
This is based on Robin Moore's novel

00:16:14.773 --> 00:16:16.298
called "The Green Berets."

00:16:16.298 --> 00:16:18.064
In this particular scene,

00:16:18.064 --> 00:16:20.697
this Green Beret team is going to kidnap

00:16:20.697 --> 00:16:24.755
this PAVN colonel or general,

00:16:24.755 --> 00:16:26.490
but as far as units working together,

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.770
like with the Green
Berets, you know, maybe.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:32.010
1965, '66, '67,

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:35.790
there's probably more the
Americans working on their own.

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:39.900
There would always be an ARVN
liaison with bigger units,

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:42.840
like probably at the
battalion or regimental level.

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:44.282
Certainly at the division level.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:51.600
The game must have been engrossing,

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:53.520
because they don't hear anybody else.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.340
You've got six or seven guys
coming through with combat gear

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:59.490
and combat boots on, on a
pier-and-beam wood floor

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:02.910
that apparently doesn't squeak,
and they're none the wiser.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:05.007
It's just totally ludicrous.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:13.950
And now we have to do
this in dramatic fashion

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:16.860
and we have to rappel
off the second story.

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:18.540
Because apparently the
guys playing mah-jongg

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:21.015
still are not alerted
that something's going on.

00:17:28.529 --> 00:17:30.390
Ah, everything goes according to plan.

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:32.040
So, the trip wire,

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:35.730
is that a common thing
for special forces to do?

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:38.940
I'd say no, because you want
to be able to get out of there

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:41.640
and get away quickly if
you're on some sort of mission

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.380
anywhere close to like this one.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:45.960
But even then, do you have time to do it?

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:49.050
That's more of a thing you
set for a defensive position.

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:50.250
Can I have a rating of:

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.800
Should never have been
filmed in the first place?

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:54.240
Zero. Totally.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:55.290
And I hate to do that

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:57.690
because part of me likes
some John Wayne films.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:00.478
But this one, ah, no.

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:09.450
This scene is depicting
the battle for Huế city,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:13.260
which is part of the great
Tết Offensive in January 1968.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.710
What you just saw with
the tank going forward,

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:18.270
the Marines going behind the tank,

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:19.980
yeah, that's good policy.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.130
But then you see all the firing.
So it's been pre-sighted.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:26.040
But what's funny to me is
then the Marines get up

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:28.380
and immediately go into that area

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:31.980
where they just pre-sighted,
pre-ranged fire.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:34.320
So if I was the bad guy, the enemy,

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:35.760
I would've fired again

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.890
while the Marines were all right there.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:39.024
But they don't do that.

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:48.030
Now, to be clear, this looks
nothing like Huế city at all.

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:50.580
Huế city, there's no
tall buildings like that.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.100
It's very dense buildings.

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:55.140
There's not a lot of maneuver room.

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:56.700
But here it looks really open.

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.610
In part this is because they
filmed this outside of London

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:01.200
at Beckton Gas Works.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:02.940
The one thing they do get right,

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:06.750
because it's outside of
London, it's overcast.

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:08.190
So during the battle for Huế,

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:10.110
it was overcast for most of the time,

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:12.501
which made it difficult
to get air support in.

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:18.810
Now, if you notice at
the very beginning there,

00:19:18.810 --> 00:19:21.540
when the firing starts to
come from the building,

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:23.370
it's not a single sniper,

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:26.490
it's several machine guns set up there.

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:28.260
A sniper would've fired a couple shots

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:30.060
and got the heck out of there.

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:33.510
These guys are in place, so
it's hard for them to move.

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:35.400
So that's why the Marines respond

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.050
with just blasting the area
with fire, trying to hit them.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:44.460
All right, so there he
is taking a picture.

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:47.410
It was not uncommon for

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:50.535
soldiers to have nice cameras.

00:19:50.535 --> 00:19:52.710
Because they get R&amp;R sometimes.

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:53.801
They can go to Saigon,

00:19:53.801 --> 00:19:55.984
or Ho Chi Minh City, I should say, now.

00:19:55.984 --> 00:19:58.890
If they're officers,
they can go to Hong Kong

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:00.450
or someplace like that,

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:04.892
and you could buy really nice
cameras for not very much.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:11.520
And this would've been a PAVN sniper.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:13.920
The PAVN snipers are going
to go after specific targets.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:17.310
VC snipers tended to use the tactic

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:20.280
just to cause chaos and fear.

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:22.080
So you randomly hit somebody.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:25.350
You see a guy on a radio
headset, talking on the radio,

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:26.610
that's probably an officer.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:27.900
You're an easy target.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:29.425
The visual part of it,

00:20:29.425 --> 00:20:32.500
the setting of it is, to me, all wrong.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:35.250
Other than the overcast
skies, that is realistic.

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:37.667
I don't know. I'd give it probably a six.

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:45.540
I don't think he needs binoculars

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:48.750
to see that the enemy is
approaching, but it's a good touch.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.933
The scene is really about

00:20:51.933 --> 00:20:54.600
the 1972 attack on Quảng Trị.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:59.130
So the PAVN have occupied
Quảng Trị city and the citadel,

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:02.254
and the ARVN are trying to take it back.

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:10.950
And the ARVN, of course, they've
got all the American stuff.

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:14.670
So their uniforms were
American style. That's spot-on.

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.800
So, you gotta remember, this is 1972,

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:18.690
following the Easter Offensive.

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.130
So by that stage,

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:21.240
even though the United States

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:22.680
is technically still in the war,

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:25.410
we're not doing a lot of
combat activity at that point.

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:28.068
Most of our troops are
out of Vietnam by then.

00:21:36.427 --> 00:21:37.435
Equipment's good.

00:21:37.435 --> 00:21:41.100
They've got AK-47s, some RPGs there.

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:43.080
So, the PAVN unit that's depicted here

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:45.390
is a basic infantry unit.

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:48.930
Yes, by this time in
'72, they've got tanks.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:51.180
They're a conventional force.

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:53.970
The only thing they don't
really have a lot of is air.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:56.445
You did see initially

00:21:56.445 --> 00:22:00.303
the forward artillery guy calling in

00:22:00.303 --> 00:22:02.940
the artillery strikes,
which didn't do anything,

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.250
but there's no depiction of him

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:08.160
asking for an adjustment of fire to hit,

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:09.450
which, I think in reality

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.100
there would've been a fire adjustment

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:13.980
and they would've kept
firing on those APCs

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:18.180
until they got really close
to the PAVN perimeter there.

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.600
So that part of it I
think is not accurate.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.070
I like this movie.

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:26.460
Its production value is not great,

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:29.460
but the storyline and
the period it's set in

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:33.742
and the way they depict
soldiers and everything,

00:22:33.742 --> 00:22:36.159
I like it. And this battle scene,

00:22:36.159 --> 00:22:40.230
even though the ARVN guys are
out there exposed and whatnot,

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:42.480
I gotta give it a six or seven, actually.

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:44.040
I kind of like this one.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:47.160
So, I have two favorite Vietnam films.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:51.660
One is from the early '70s,
called "Go Tell the Spartans."

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:52.980
And it's early in the war,

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:54.360
it's in the advisory phase,

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:58.080
and it's kind of foreshadowing

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:01.020
the problems that are going to arise.

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:02.797
The other one I like a lot is actually

00:23:02.797 --> 00:23:04.350
"Good Morning, Vietnam."

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:06.570
Hey, thanks so much for
watching. I enjoyed it.

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:07.590
I hope you enjoyed it.

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:10.540
If you want to watch some
more, click on the next video.

